<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<CustomField xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <fullName>Disposition_Driver__c</fullName>
    <!--
        WHY THIS IS A NEW FIELD AND NOT A REUSE OF User.Deal_Driver__c (decision D15/Q1).
        This comment sits INSIDE the root element deliberately: a comment above the root
        breaks `sf` at source conversion with "unable to find matching parent xml file".

        The disposition gate is TWO-FACTOR, exactly like the acquisitions deal-driver gate
        it mirrors: the service reads this field WITH USER_MODE, so access requires BOTH
        (a) FLS read on this field, granted by the Disposition_Driver permission set, AND
        (b) the field value being true on the running user's own User record. A USER_MODE
        read by a principal without the FLS THROWS rather than returning null, and the
        service converts that throw to false - which is what makes (a) a real factor and
        not documentation.

        Reusing Deal_Driver__c would have collapsed two different populations into one:
        the acquisitions analyst who drives deals and the analyst who drives sale
        processes are not the same person, and a membership-only or shared-flag gate
        widens a live authorization boundary silently for every existing holder. See the
        ARCHITECTURE.md note on why the Opportunity deal-action gate is deliberately NOT
        the Lead-style membership check - the same reasoning applies here.
    -->
    <defaultValue>false</defaultValue>
    <description>When checked, the user sees the disposition-driving quick actions (stage advances on Disposition). The analyst who runs sale processes - not approvers.</description>
    <inlineHelpText>Check this for the analyst who drives disposition stage advances. Access also needs the Disposition Driver permission set; both factors are required.</inlineHelpText>
    <label>Disposition Driver</label>
    <trackFeedHistory>false</trackFeedHistory>
    <trackHistory>false</trackHistory>
    <type>Checkbox</type>
</CustomField>
